1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,000
A full tour of the app, chapter by chapter.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:08,250
Install it, and look around with sample data.

3
00:00:08,250 --> 00:00:14,750
Fishamongus! is a fishing log that lives on your phone: what you caught, where, on what, and in what conditions. No account, nothing uploaded.

4
00:00:14,750 --> 00:00:21,500
New here? Load a month of sample fishing to look around. In Washington it’s set around Anderson Island. Clear it in one tap from the banner.

5
00:00:21,500 --> 00:00:24,750
Conditions, bite times and suggestions for where you are.

6
00:00:24,750 --> 00:00:31,750
The Now screen opens first: weather, wind and gusts, the pressure trend, the tide and its strength, sunrise and sunset, water temperature and waves.

7
00:00:31,750 --> 00:00:38,750
In Washington, saltwater places show nearby public shellfish beaches from the Department of Health map: open, closed or conditional, and why.

8
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:45,250
Solunar bite times are worked out on the phone from the moon’s position, with a rating for the day and the next major or minor period.

9
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:52,500
It suggests the lures that have caught you the most fish in conditions like these – sky, pressure, tide, time of day – and shows your next planned trip.

10
00:00:52,500 --> 00:00:55,750
Log a catch in one tap, or just say it.

11
00:00:55,750 --> 00:01:02,250
Quick catch saves your usual fish and lure in one tap, with GPS, weather and tide filled in. Add the details later if you want.

12
00:01:02,250 --> 00:01:10,250
Hands busy? Tap Say it and say what you caught – “two coho, kept, on a spoon”. It saves the fish, lure, count and kept or released, with Undo if it misheard.

13
00:01:10,250 --> 00:01:17,000
The microphone in the catch form fills in the fish, lure, count and released or kept for you to check before saving.

14
00:01:17,000 --> 00:01:20,250
Time your fishing and track the lure on your line.

15
00:01:20,250 --> 00:01:26,250
Tap Start fishing when your line goes in. The app times the session and keeps track of the lure on your line.

16
00:01:26,250 --> 00:01:32,750
Switch lures with one tap. Every catch in the session gets the right lure, and Stats can show fish per hour for each one.

17
00:01:32,750 --> 00:01:39,000
End the session when you’re done. It keeps the time on the water, the lures you used and the fish you caught on each.

18
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:42,250
Everything about a catch, most of it filled in for you.

19
00:01:42,250 --> 00:01:48,250
Tap + to log a catch. Add photos, then search more than 280 fish and shellfish by name.

20
00:01:48,250 --> 00:01:55,750
Fish and shellfish, freshwater and saltwater: the buttons shorten the list. Fish are grouped by type – every bass under Bass – with the kind in a second menu.

21
00:01:55,750 --> 00:02:02,250
The lure from your session is already on the form. GPS, weather, pressure and the matching spot are filled in automatically.

22
00:02:02,250 --> 00:02:08,750
No scale? Enter the length and the app estimates a typical weight for that fish, in pounds and ounces or kilograms.

23
00:02:08,750 --> 00:02:15,250
Log several of the same fish at once, and mark them kept or released. Kept fish count toward your daily limits.

24
00:02:15,250 --> 00:02:20,500
Saved. “Log another” opens a new form with the same fish, lure and spot.

25
00:02:20,500 --> 00:02:23,750
Crab, shrimp, clams and oysters, with the rules that go with them.

26
00:02:23,750 --> 00:02:31,250
Tap Shellfish to log crab, shrimp and prawns, clams, oysters, mussels, scallops, squid and crayfish. The lure menu becomes gear: pots, ring nets, clam guns, shovels.

27
00:02:31,250 --> 00:02:38,750
Crab gets sex, hard or soft shell and shell width, and pots get a count and soak time. A crab you can’t keep under your limits is flagged before you save.

28
00:02:38,750 --> 00:02:46,000
Log clams, oysters or mussels in Washington and the form shows the live status of the beaches nearby. Biotoxins aren’t destroyed by cooking.

29
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:49,250
Every catch, searchable, with the conditions it was caught in.

30
00:02:49,250 --> 00:02:55,250
Every catch is saved with its photos, lure, place and conditions, grouped by day with a count for each.

31
00:02:55,250 --> 00:03:01,250
Search by fish, lure, colour, notes or who caught it, and filter by date, spot and species.

32
00:03:01,250 --> 00:03:07,250
Swipe a catch left to edit it, log the same again, or delete it – with Undo for a few seconds.

33
00:03:07,250 --> 00:03:13,750
Open a catch to see everything: photos full screen, size, lure, the spot on a map, and the weather and tide when it bit.

34
00:03:13,750 --> 00:03:19,500
Sections fold away and still show their key values, so the page stays short.

35
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:22,750
What actually works for you, in plain words.

36
00:03:22,750 --> 00:03:30,250
Stats sums up your log in plain words – your best lure and condition, best time of day, fastest lure – always with the number of fish behind it.

37
00:03:30,250 --> 00:03:37,250
See which lures work in each sky, pressure trend, wind, tide stage, moon phase and solunar period, and where your fish came from.

38
00:03:37,250 --> 00:03:43,750
Timed sessions give fish per hour for each lure – a fairer test than raw counts, which favour the lure you use most.

39
00:03:43,750 --> 00:03:51,250
In Washington, Stats counts the salmon, steelhead, sturgeon and halibut you’ve kept this license year, and reminds you before each card is due.

40
00:03:51,250 --> 00:03:58,500
Switch Stats to shellfish: catch per pot-hour for each gear and bait, and your kept Dungeness for the summer and winter crab cards.

41
00:03:58,500 --> 00:04:01,750
Your places, the shellfish beaches near them, and river flows.

42
00:04:01,750 --> 00:04:08,250
Save your spots. Catches made there link automatically, and the colours show where fish came in the last 30 and 90 days.

43
00:04:08,250 --> 00:04:14,250
Each spot has its own stats: best lures, best conditions and recent catches.

44
00:04:14,250 --> 00:04:20,250
Saltwater spots in Washington show the shellfish beaches nearby, so you know before you dig.

45
00:04:20,250 --> 00:04:27,250
On rivers, the Now screen shows the nearest USGS stream gauge: the flow in cubic feet per second and whether it’s rising or falling.

46
00:04:27,250 --> 00:04:34,000
River spots show their gauge too. Catches made near a gauge save the flow, and Stats adds River flow as a condition.

47
00:04:34,000 --> 00:04:37,250
Forecast, tides, bite times and a packing list for each trip.

48
00:04:37,250 --> 00:04:43,750
Plan trips ahead. Upcoming trips are listed with their date and place, and each one keeps its last forecast for when you have no signal.

49
00:04:43,750 --> 00:04:50,750
Choose what the trip is for – fishing, crabbing, clamming or shrimping – then where and when: a saved spot, your location or a point on the map.

50
00:04:50,750 --> 00:04:57,250
The trip plan shows the forecast hour by hour, with your best hours marked from the conditions you’ve caught fish in.

51
00:04:57,250 --> 00:05:04,250
Trips add NOAA tides for the day. Clamming trips also list the daylight minus tides for the next two weeks – the best times to dig.

52
00:05:04,250 --> 00:05:10,250
First light, sunrise, sunset and the moon, plus the solunar bite times that fall during your trip.

53
00:05:10,250 --> 00:05:16,750
Each kind of trip gets its own packing list – the crab gauge, the shovel, the headlamp for evening low tides – ready to tick off.

54
00:05:16,750 --> 00:05:23,500
Shellfish trips show the beach status near where you’re going, with links to the Shellfish Safety Map and Washington’s rules.

55
00:05:23,500 --> 00:05:26,750
Daily limits, kept-today counts and Washington catch record cards.

56
00:05:26,750 --> 00:05:33,250
Set daily limits for any fish or shellfish. One tap adds Washington’s Puget Sound crab limits and the clam and oyster limits.

57
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:40,250
Each limit can carry its rules – males only, hard shell, minimum size, clams counted together – and the app counts what you keep each day.

58
00:05:40,250 --> 00:05:46,500
As you log, the form shows how many you’ve kept today against your limit, and warns before you go over.

59
00:05:46,500 --> 00:05:49,750
Tackle box, sharing, backups and night mode.

60
00:05:49,750 --> 00:05:56,250
Keep your real lures in a tackle box – type, name, colour, size and a photo – and pick them with one tap when you log a catch.

61
00:05:56,250 --> 00:06:03,250
Settings gives one-tap links for your usual catches. On Android, press and hold the app icon for Say a catch, Log a catch and Start fishing.

62
00:06:03,250 --> 00:06:10,250
Merge a fishing partner’s backup, or your old phone’s, into your log. Their catches keep their name, and nothing is duplicated.

63
00:06:10,250 --> 00:06:17,250
Your log stays on your phone. Automatic backups after each session keep a copy safe, and a backup file moves everything to a new phone.

64
00:06:17,250 --> 00:06:24,000
Fishing before dawn? Night mode turns the whole screen dim red to protect your night vision. Large text is one tap away too.

65
00:06:24,000 --> 00:06:31,000
Open the link in Safari or Chrome, then add it to your home screen: Share → Add to Home Screen, or the menu → Install app.

